# Story2Code — Rules File

These rules are given verbatim to every LLM at both the spec-generation stage and the
full-project-generation stage. They exist for one reason: the pre-written unit tests are
compiled directly against your generated code (white-box testing), so a small set of names
must be exact and predictable across every model and every run. Everything else about the
project — architecture, folder layout, extra layers, DI setup, coding style — is entirely
your choice.

## Target stack (this study)

- Language: C#
- SDK: .NET 8 (`net8.0` target framework)
- Project type: ASP.NET Core Web API (`Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web`)

## Naming contract (MUST follow exactly — case-sensitive)

1. **Main project.** The implementation project's file name, `<AssemblyName>`, and
   `<RootNamespace>` must all be exactly `Implementation` (i.e. `Implementation.csproj`,
   namespace root `Implementation`).

2. **Data models.** Define every data model class described in the user story's "Data Model"
   section in namespace `Implementation.Models`, using the exact class name and exact property
   names/types given in that section. Do not rename, pluralize, or reinterpret them.

3. **Service contract.** For the method named `<MethodName>` in the user story's "API
   Interface" section:
   - Define an interface `Implementation.Services.I<MethodName>Service` with a single public
     method `<MethodName>` matching the parameters (name + type) and return type/shape
     described in the "Parameters" and "Response" sections.
   - The method must be **synchronous** — its return type is the plain response shape itself
     (e.g. `IEnumerable<RecyclingFacility>`), never wrapped in `Task<...>` and never suffixed
     `Async`. The pre-written tests call it directly and do not `await`. If you need
     async-style internals, do the awaiting inside the method body and return the resolved
     result.
   - Define a public class `Implementation.Services.<MethodName>Service` implementing that
     interface. Its constructor must accept the full candidate dataset as a single parameter of
     type `IEnumerable<TDataModel>`, where `TDataModel` is the data model class the method
     returns (e.g. `IEnumerable<RecyclingFacility>`). The class must operate on the data it is
     given — **do not hardcode or seed any mock/sample data inside this class.** The pre-written
     tests construct it directly with their own known dataset (e.g.
     `new GetNearbyRecyclingFacilitiesService(testFacilities)`), so it must have no parameterless
     constructor and no internal fallback data source.
   - This is a build-time-only requirement for the live app: your `Program.cs`/DI wiring must
     still compile (e.g. register a factory that supplies your own seed data for a live-running
     instance), but the pipeline never executes the live app, so what that wiring actually does
     at runtime is entirely up to you.

   Example: for a `GetNearbyRecyclingFacilities` method, this means
   `Implementation.Services.IGetNearbyRecyclingFacilitiesService` and
   `Implementation.Services.GetNearbyRecyclingFacilitiesService`, with
   `IEnumerable<RecyclingFacility> GetNearbyRecyclingFacilities(string zipCode)` — not
   `Task<IEnumerable<RecyclingFacility>> GetNearbyRecyclingFacilitiesAsync(string zipCode)` —
   and a constructor shaped `GetNearbyRecyclingFacilitiesService(IEnumerable<RecyclingFacility> facilities)`.

4. **Required parameters & empty results.**
   - For any parameter marked `Required: Yes` in the "Parameters" table, the service method
     must throw `System.ArgumentException` (or a subclass, e.g. `ArgumentNullException`) when
     the caller passes `null`, an empty string, or a whitespace-only string for that parameter.
     Do not silently accept or default such values.
   - For a valid, well-formed input where no matching data exists, return an empty collection
     — never `null`, never throw — consistent with the documented HTTP 200 OK response shape.

5. **Controller.** Wire an ASP.NET Core controller that calls the service above to serve the
   documented HTTP method + route. The controller's internal name/structure is unconstrained —
   only the service class and data model names above are load-bearing for tests.

## What you may design freely

- Overall project/folder structure beyond the pinned namespaces above.
- Additional layers (repositories, DTOs, mappers, validators), as long as the pinned
  interface/class described in rule 3 is what the tests call.
- Dependency injection wiring, middleware, logging, error handling.
- Any NuGet packages needed, declared in `Implementation.csproj`.

## Output format

- Stage 1 (spec generation): produce a single markdown document describing your intended
  architecture, file list, and how it satisfies the naming contract above. Do not include full
  source code at this stage.
- Stage 2 (full project generation): produce every file needed to build the project, each as a
  fenced code block preceded by a line of the exact form:

  `// FILE: <relative/path/from/project/root>`

  Include the `.csproj` file itself. Do not include the test project — that is supplied
  separately and merged in by the pipeline.
